{"id":"refine_20260929114513284","summary":"Saved a reusable ARC strategy for stamping a seed motif at isolated marker cells.","rationale":"The strategy was validated on task-b8a5b6a1: comparing the demo examples revealed that the non-background cells of a seed motif are copied at each marker using a consistent anchor and orientation, while background-colored cells remain transparent.","expectedOutcome":"Future ARC tasks with isolated markers and a distinct seed motif can be solved more reliably by inferring and validating the motif's anchor, offsets, colors, and orientation from demonstrations.","appliedEdits":[{"action":"create","kind":"memory","id":"infer_motif_stamping_from_arc_demonstrations","title":"Infer motif stamping from ARC demonstrations","content":"For ARC-style grids with many isolated marker cells and one distinct colored seed motif, compare demonstrations to determine whether the motif is stamped at each marker. Preserve the seed, align each marker to the inferred anchor pixel (often the motif center), and copy the motif's non-background cells using the same offsets and colors. Treat background-colored motif cells as transparent. Validate the orientation and anchor against the demonstrations before submitting. This approach solved task-b8a5b6a1: each 1-marker received the 3x3 motif aligned to its central 5, including the motif's 8/5/0 pattern.","metadata":{"scope":"global"},"reason":"The successful solution demonstrates a reusable transformation pattern and a concrete way to infer and validate it from examples.","after":{"id":"infer_motif_stamping_from_arc_demonstrations","kind":"memory","title":"Infer motif stamping from ARC demonstrations","content":"For ARC-style grids with many isolated marker cells and one distinct colored seed motif, compare demonstrations to determine whether the motif is stamped at each marker. Preserve the seed, align each marker to the inferred anchor pixel (often the motif center), and copy the motif's non-background cells using the same offsets and colors. Treat background-colored motif cells as transparent. Validate the orientation and anchor against the demonstrations before submitting. This approach solved task-b8a5b6a1: each 1-marker received the 3x3 motif aligned to its central 5, including the motif's 8/5/0 pattern.","path":"general","scope":"global","reference":{},"arguments":{},"metadata":{"scope":"global"},"source":"refine","created_at":"2026-09-29T11:45:17.668Z","updated_at":"2026-09-29T11:45:17.668Z","version":1},"applied":true}],"harnessStatePath":"/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-primeagentlearner-1790682263/attempts/f584a00acdda4d60937d1e953c54734b/system/state/agent/harness/harness_state.json","scope":"global"}
{"id":"refine_20260929114648200","summary":"Updated the global ARC motif-stamping memory with a validated rule for matching and copying seed motifs onto zero-valued cells.","rationale":"The trajectory confirms the strategy across three motif shapes and noisy backgrounds: preserve the seed, find translations whose exact footprint is all zeros, and recolor only those cells with the seed color.","expectedOutcome":"Future ARC solutions should more reliably distinguish exact zero-footprint matches from matches against the dominant noise color; validate by checking the seed footprint, all translated matches, and unchanged non-target cells.","appliedEdits":[{"action":"update","kind":"memory","id":"infer_motif_stamping_from_arc_demonstrations","title":"Infer motif stamping from ARC demonstrations (general, v2)","content":"For ARC-style noisy grids, inspect demonstrations for a small connected seed motif in a distinct non-background color. Infer its exact footprint as offsets from an anchor, its color, and the target-cell condition from input-output differences. A common rule is to find every translation where every footprint cell lies on a zero-valued input cell, then recolor only those footprint cells with the seed color. Preserve the original seed and all other cells, including nonzero noise cells. Do not assume the dominant background color is the target match condition: verify from demonstrations whether matches require zeros. Validate the inferred rule against all demonstrated changes before applying it to the test grid. Confirmed examples: a 3-cell V of 6 stamped onto zero Vs in a 5-noise grid; a 7-cell irregular 5 motif copied onto matching zero footprints in a 9-noise grid; and a vertical 3-cell 1 motif stamped at every vertical triple of zeros in a 5-noise test grid.","metadata":{"scope":"global","evidence":"Validated across ARC demonstrations and task-62e8a5ef"},"reason":"Adds demonstrated precision about exact motif footprints, zero-cell matching, noise preservation, and checking the inferred target condition.","before":{"id":"infer_motif_stamping_from_arc_demonstrations","kind":"memory","title":"Infer motif stamping from ARC demonstrations","content":"For ARC-style grids with many isolated marker cells and one distinct colored seed motif, compare demonstrations to determine whether the motif is stamped at each marker. Preserve the seed, align each marker to the inferred anchor pixel (often the motif center), and copy the motif's non-background cells using the same offsets and colors. Treat background-colored motif cells as transparent. Validate the orientation and anchor against the demonstrations before submitting. This approach solved task-b8a5b6a1: each 1-marker received the 3x3 motif aligned to its central 5, including the motif's 8/5/0 pattern.","path":"general","scope":"global","reference":{},"arguments":{},"metadata":{"scope":"global"},"source":"refine","created_at":"2026-09-29T11:45:17.668Z","updated_at":"2026-09-29T11:45:17.668Z","version":1},"after":{"id":"infer_motif_stamping_from_arc_demonstrations","kind":"memory","title":"Infer motif stamping from ARC demonstrations (general, v2)","content":"For ARC-style noisy grids, inspect demonstrations for a small connected seed motif in a distinct non-background color. Infer its exact footprint as offsets from an anchor, its color, and the target-cell condition from input-output differences. A common rule is to find every translation where every footprint cell lies on a zero-valued input cell, then recolor only those footprint cells with the seed color. Preserve the original seed and all other cells, including nonzero noise cells. Do not assume the dominant background color is the target match condition: verify from demonstrations whether matches require zeros. Validate the inferred rule against all demonstrated changes before applying it to the test grid. Confirmed examples: a 3-cell V of 6 stamped onto zero Vs in a 5-noise grid; a 7-cell irregular 5 motif copied onto matching zero footprints in a 9-noise grid; and a vertical 3-cell 1 motif stamped at every vertical triple of zeros in a 5-noise test grid.","path":"general","scope":"global","reference":{},"arguments":{},"metadata":{"scope":"global","evidence":"Validated across ARC demonstrations and task-62e8a5ef"},"source":"refine","created_at":"2026-09-29T11:45:17.668Z","updated_at":"2026-09-29T11:46:56.905Z","version":2},"applied":true}],"harnessStatePath":"/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-primeagentlearner-1790682263/attempts/f584a00acdda4d60937d1e953c54734b/system/state/agent/harness/harness_state.json","scope":"global"}
{"id":"refine_20260929114757420","summary":"Updated the global ARC motif-stamping memory with a second validated example and clarified fixed-orientation zero-footprint matching.","rationale":"The task was solved correctly by identifying the 1-colored seed motif, scanning its fixed-orientation footprint for placements whose motif-offset cells are all zero, and changing only those cells; the earlier demonstration validated the same rule with a different background and motif color.","expectedOutcome":"Future ARC solutions should more reliably identify exact motif offsets and apply them to all valid zero-cell placements while leaving surrounding and non-target cells unchanged; validate by checking every in-bounds anchor and comparing the transformed cells against the rule.","appliedEdits":[{"action":"update","kind":"memory","id":"infer_motif_stamping_from_arc_demonstrations","title":"Infer motif stamping from ARC demonstrations (general, v2)","content":"For ARC-style noisy grids, inspect demonstrations for a compact connected seed motif in a distinct non-background color. Infer its exact footprint as offsets from an anchor and identify the target-cell condition from input-output differences. When demonstrations show stamping onto zero-valued cells, scan every in-bounds anchor using the seed's fixed orientation: a placement matches exactly when every cell at a colored seed-footprint offset is zero in the original grid. Change only those footprint cells to the seed color; preserve every other cell. Cells surrounding the footprint are unrestricted, and the dominant noise/background color is not the matching target. Do not add rotations or reflections unless demonstrations support them. Check all anchors, including possible overlapping placements, against the original grid. This rule has been validated with a 3-colored seed on background 5 and a 1-colored seed on background 9.","metadata":{"scope":"global"},"reason":"A second correctly solved instance confirms and sharpens the reusable motif-stamping rule across different noise and motif colors.","before":{"id":"infer_motif_stamping_from_arc_demonstrations","kind":"memory","title":"Infer motif stamping from ARC demonstrations (general, v2)","content":"For ARC-style noisy grids, inspect demonstrations for a small connected seed motif in a distinct non-background color. Infer its exact footprint as offsets from an anchor, its color, and the target-cell condition from input-output differences. A common rule is to find every translation where every footprint cell lies on a zero-valued input cell, then recolor only those footprint cells with the seed color. Preserve the original seed and all other cells, including nonzero noise cells. Do not assume the dominant background color is the target match condition: verify from demonstrations whether matches require zeros. Validate the inferred rule against all demonstrated changes before applying it to the test grid. Confirmed examples: a 3-cell V of 6 stamped onto zero Vs in a 5-noise grid; a 7-cell irregular 5 motif copied onto matching zero footprints in a 9-noise grid; and a vertical 3-cell 1 motif stamped at every vertical triple of zeros in a 5-noise test grid.","path":"general","scope":"global","reference":{},"arguments":{},"metadata":{"scope":"global","evidence":"Validated across ARC demonstrations and task-62e8a5ef"},"source":"refine","created_at":"2026-09-29T11:45:17.668Z","updated_at":"2026-09-29T11:46:56.905Z","version":2},"after":{"id":"infer_motif_stamping_from_arc_demonstrations","kind":"memory","title":"Infer motif stamping from ARC demonstrations (general, v2)","content":"For ARC-style noisy grids, inspect demonstrations for a compact connected seed motif in a distinct non-background color. Infer its exact footprint as offsets from an anchor and identify the target-cell condition from input-output differences. When demonstrations show stamping onto zero-valued cells, scan every in-bounds anchor using the seed's fixed orientation: a placement matches exactly when every cell at a colored seed-footprint offset is zero in the original grid. Change only those footprint cells to the seed color; preserve every other cell. Cells surrounding the footprint are unrestricted, and the dominant noise/background color is not the matching target. Do not add rotations or reflections unless demonstrations support them. Check all anchors, including possible overlapping placements, against the original grid. This rule has been validated with a 3-colored seed on background 5 and a 1-colored seed on background 9.","path":"general","scope":"global","reference":{},"arguments":{},"metadata":{"scope":"global"},"source":"refine","created_at":"2026-09-29T11:45:17.668Z","updated_at":"2026-09-29T11:48:02.997Z","version":3},"applied":true}],"harnessStatePath":"/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-primeagentlearner-1790682263/attempts/f584a00acdda4d60937d1e953c54734b/system/state/agent/harness/harness_state.json","scope":"global"}
{"id":"refine_20260929115517644","summary":"Added a global memory for the demonstrated ARC elbow-coloring task family and its validation checks.","rationale":"Eight demonstrations and the solved test support identifying a large monochrome elbow as the paint-color cue, recoloring a selected component, and using shape duplicates and position relative to the elbow to disambiguate the target. The trajectory also exposed a malformed row-length submission, so dimension validation is a useful procedural caution.","expectedOutcome":"Future instances in this task family should more reliably identify the elbow color and target component while avoiding unsupported assumptions about which duplicate to recolor; validate the inferred rule against demonstrations and check every output row length before submission.","appliedEdits":[{"action":"create","kind":"memory","id":"arc_elbow_guided_component_recoloring","title":"Infer component recoloring from large elbow-shaped color cues","content":"In this demonstrated ARC task family, a large monochrome L/elbow-shaped object supplies the paint color: one other entire component is recolored to the elbow's color. To identify the target, compare component shapes (including duplicate motifs) and their positions relative to the elbow's corner and arms; use the demonstrations to infer which component is selected. Often the selected component is spatially associated with the quadrant diagonally opposite the elbow's arms, but do not treat that as an unconditional rule or assume the matching copy itself is the target. In the solved task-7347b058 test, the color-2 elbow had arms right and down; the upper-left zero motif matched a color-8 motif in the opposite lower-right, and the upper-left motif was recolored to 2 while the color-8 copy remained unchanged. Change only the inferred target component and preserve everything else. Before submitting, validate output dimensions and every row length; a malformed row caused invalid-grid attempts in this instance. Evidence: eight demonstrations and the solved test showed selected components recolored to the elbow's color.","metadata":{"scope":"global","evidence":"Eight demonstrations and solved test from task-7347b058"},"reason":"Preserves the validated task-family rule and cautions against overgeneralizing the ambiguous target-selection heuristic.","after":{"id":"arc_elbow_guided_component_recoloring","kind":"memory","title":"Infer component recoloring from large elbow-shaped color cues","content":"In this demonstrated ARC task family, a large monochrome L/elbow-shaped object supplies the paint color: one other entire component is recolored to the elbow's color. To identify the target, compare component shapes (including duplicate motifs) and their positions relative to the elbow's corner and arms; use the demonstrations to infer which component is selected. Often the selected component is spatially associated with the quadrant diagonally opposite the elbow's arms, but do not treat that as an unconditional rule or assume the matching copy itself is the target. In the solved task-7347b058 test, the color-2 elbow had arms right and down; the upper-left zero motif matched a color-8 motif in the opposite lower-right, and the upper-left motif was recolored to 2 while the color-8 copy remained unchanged. Change only the inferred target component and preserve everything else. Before submitting, validate output dimensions and every row length; a malformed row caused invalid-grid attempts in this instance. Evidence: eight demonstrations and the solved test showed selected components recolored to the elbow's color.","path":"general","scope":"global","reference":{},"arguments":{},"metadata":{"scope":"global","evidence":"Eight demonstrations and solved test from task-7347b058"},"source":"refine","created_at":"2026-09-29T11:55:26.707Z","updated_at":"2026-09-29T11:55:26.707Z","version":1},"applied":true}],"harnessStatePath":"/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-primeagentlearner-1790682263/attempts/f584a00acdda4d60937d1e953c54734b/system/state/agent/harness/harness_state.json","scope":"global"}
{"id":"refine_20260929115952286","summary":"Added a global memory for the demonstrated ARC task family that fills enclosed background pockets with color 4.","rationale":"Four demonstrations show uniform-background grids with monochrome line patterns and enclosed pockets recolored to 4; the accepted test also confirms a pocket bounded partly by the grid edge can count, while the unchanged-grid attempt was incorrect.","expectedOutcome":"Future instances with similar line-and-pocket patterns should more reliably identify the target cells, including edge-bounded pockets, and leave exterior regions and other cells unchanged; validate the inferred rule against demonstrations and the full grid.","appliedEdits":[{"action":"create","kind":"memory","id":"arc_enclosed_pocket_fill_color4","title":"Fill enclosed pockets in ARC line-pattern grids","content":"For ARC-style grids with a uniform background and monochrome line or outline patterns, inspect the demonstrations for enclosed background pockets that are recolored to 4. The background and stroke colors can vary: do not assume the non-background color is 4 or that the background is 0. Demonstrations include multiple pockets and narrow interiors. Consider the grid frame when a stroke meets an edge: a pocket can be bounded partly by the frame and still count. In task-8aa4d19a, the accepted output recolored the 4x3 background pocket at rows 5–8, columns 22–24 (0-based), bounded by strokes above, left, and below and by the right grid edge. A rule that only fills background components not touching any boundary is insufficient. Infer which compartments are filled from the examples, avoid treating every edge-touching region as exterior or every bounded-looking region as a target, and preserve all other cells.","metadata":{"scope":"global"},"reason":"This captures a validated, reusable task pattern and a specific correction to the failed assumption that edge-touching background regions must remain unchanged.","after":{"id":"arc_enclosed_pocket_fill_color4","kind":"memory","title":"Fill enclosed pockets in ARC line-pattern grids","content":"For ARC-style grids with a uniform background and monochrome line or outline patterns, inspect the demonstrations for enclosed background pockets that are recolored to 4. The background and stroke colors can vary: do not assume the non-background color is 4 or that the background is 0. Demonstrations include multiple pockets and narrow interiors. Consider the grid frame when a stroke meets an edge: a pocket can be bounded partly by the frame and still count. In task-8aa4d19a, the accepted output recolored the 4x3 background pocket at rows 5–8, columns 22–24 (0-based), bounded by strokes above, left, and below and by the right grid edge. A rule that only fills background components not touching any boundary is insufficient. Infer which compartments are filled from the examples, avoid treating every edge-touching region as exterior or every bounded-looking region as a target, and preserve all other cells.","path":"general","scope":"global","reference":{},"arguments":{},"metadata":{"scope":"global"},"source":"refine","created_at":"2026-09-29T11:59:58.172Z","updated_at":"2026-09-29T11:59:58.172Z","version":1},"applied":true}],"harnessStatePath":"/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-primeagentlearner-1790682263/attempts/f584a00acdda4d60937d1e953c54734b/system/state/agent/harness/harness_state.json","scope":"global"}
{"id":"refine_20260929120026882","summary":"Updated the global enclosed-pocket memory with a validated example of a 3×3 cavity whose outline connects to a long exterior line.","rationale":"The test was solved correctly by recoloring the entire enclosed cavity to 4 while preserving the outline and exterior; the demonstration also supports filling enclosed pockets without changing unrelated cells.","expectedOutcome":"Future similar ARC tasks should better recognize that connections between an outline and exterior strokes do not invalidate an enclosed pocket; validate the cavity boundaries and leave all non-pocket cells unchanged.","appliedEdits":[{"action":"update","kind":"memory","id":"arc_enclosed_pocket_fill_color4","title":"Fill enclosed pockets in ARC line-pattern grids","content":"For ARC-style grids with a uniform background and monochrome line or outline patterns, inspect demonstrations for enclosed background pockets that are recolored to 4. The background and stroke colors can vary; infer the target color and pocket condition from the examples rather than assuming fixed input colors. A cavity may still count as enclosed even when its outline connects to a long stroke extending into the exterior. In a validated example, a 5×5 color-7 outline embedded in color 5 enclosed a 3×3 pocket; the outline connected to a separate long vertical color-7 line extending above and below, but the entire cavity was still recolored to 4. Change only the demonstrated enclosed pocket cells; preserve the outline and exterior background. Validate cavity boundaries and compare every changed cell with the inferred rule.","metadata":{"scope":"global"},"reason":"Adds a correctly solved example clarifying that an outline’s connection to an exterior stroke does not prevent filling its enclosed cavity.","before":{"id":"arc_enclosed_pocket_fill_color4","kind":"memory","title":"Fill enclosed pockets in ARC line-pattern grids","content":"For ARC-style grids with a uniform background and monochrome line or outline patterns, inspect the demonstrations for enclosed background pockets that are recolored to 4. The background and stroke colors can vary: do not assume the non-background color is 4 or that the background is 0. Demonstrations include multiple pockets and narrow interiors. Consider the grid frame when a stroke meets an edge: a pocket can be bounded partly by the frame and still count. In task-8aa4d19a, the accepted output recolored the 4x3 background pocket at rows 5–8, columns 22–24 (0-based), bounded by strokes above, left, and below and by the right grid edge. A rule that only fills background components not touching any boundary is insufficient. Infer which compartments are filled from the examples, avoid treating every edge-touching region as exterior or every bounded-looking region as a target, and preserve all other cells.","path":"general","scope":"global","reference":{},"arguments":{},"metadata":{"scope":"global"},"source":"refine","created_at":"2026-09-29T11:59:58.172Z","updated_at":"2026-09-29T11:59:58.172Z","version":1},"after":{"id":"arc_enclosed_pocket_fill_color4","kind":"memory","title":"Fill enclosed pockets in ARC line-pattern grids","content":"For ARC-style grids with a uniform background and monochrome line or outline patterns, inspect demonstrations for enclosed background pockets that are recolored to 4. The background and stroke colors can vary; infer the target color and pocket condition from the examples rather than assuming fixed input colors. A cavity may still count as enclosed even when its outline connects to a long stroke extending into the exterior. In a validated example, a 5×5 color-7 outline embedded in color 5 enclosed a 3×3 pocket; the outline connected to a separate long vertical color-7 line extending above and below, but the entire cavity was still recolored to 4. Change only the demonstrated enclosed pocket cells; preserve the outline and exterior background. Validate cavity boundaries and compare every changed cell with the inferred rule.","path":"general","scope":"global","reference":{},"arguments":{},"metadata":{"scope":"global"},"source":"refine","created_at":"2026-09-29T11:59:58.172Z","updated_at":"2026-09-29T12:00:32.537Z","version":2},"applied":true}],"harnessStatePath":"/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-primeagentlearner-1790682263/attempts/f584a00acdda4d60937d1e953c54734b/system/state/agent/harness/harness_state.json","scope":"global"}
{"id":"refine_20260929120248000","summary":"Updated the global ARC motif-stamping memory with a validated fixed-orientation footprint-matching rule.","rationale":"The task was solved correctly on the first submission by translating the seed’s colored-cell footprint to every position where its footprint landed entirely on zero cells; gaps within the seed’s bounding box did not constrain matches.","expectedOutcome":"Future motif-stamping solutions should handle sparse footprints accurately, including bounding-box gaps and overlapping matches; validate by checking every in-bounds translation and confirming all non-footprint cells remain unchanged.","appliedEdits":[{"action":"update","kind":"memory","id":"infer_motif_stamping_from_arc_demonstrations","title":"Infer motif stamping from ARC demonstrations","content":"For noisy ARC grids, identify the rare non-background seed color and record the exact coordinates of its colored cells as offsets from an anchor. Scan every in-bounds translation in the same fixed orientation. A translation matches when every footprint offset lands on a target-background zero cell; unoccupied cells inside the footprint's bounding box do not constrain the match. Recolor the matched footprint cells to the seed color, taking the union if matches overlap. Preserve all other cells, including noise, nonzero background, and the original seed. Infer the target condition and colors from demonstrations rather than assuming them. Validated across examples with seed colors 2, 4, and 8, including a 3-cell L footprint; verify by checking all candidate placements and that only matching footprint cells change.","metadata":{"scope":"global"},"reason":"This instance confirms that footprint matching uses only the seed's colored cells, not every cell in its bounding box, and that stamping this rule solved the test correctly.","before":{"id":"infer_motif_stamping_from_arc_demonstrations","kind":"memory","title":"Infer motif stamping from ARC demonstrations (general, v2)","content":"For ARC-style noisy grids, inspect demonstrations for a compact connected seed motif in a distinct non-background color. Infer its exact footprint as offsets from an anchor and identify the target-cell condition from input-output differences. When demonstrations show stamping onto zero-valued cells, scan every in-bounds anchor using the seed's fixed orientation: a placement matches exactly when every cell at a colored seed-footprint offset is zero in the original grid. Change only those footprint cells to the seed color; preserve every other cell. Cells surrounding the footprint are unrestricted, and the dominant noise/background color is not the matching target. Do not add rotations or reflections unless demonstrations support them. Check all anchors, including possible overlapping placements, against the original grid. This rule has been validated with a 3-colored seed on background 5 and a 1-colored seed on background 9.","path":"general","scope":"global","reference":{},"arguments":{},"metadata":{"scope":"global"},"source":"refine","created_at":"2026-09-29T11:45:17.668Z","updated_at":"2026-09-29T11:48:02.997Z","version":3},"after":{"id":"infer_motif_stamping_from_arc_demonstrations","kind":"memory","title":"Infer motif stamping from ARC demonstrations","content":"For noisy ARC grids, identify the rare non-background seed color and record the exact coordinates of its colored cells as offsets from an anchor. Scan every in-bounds translation in the same fixed orientation. A translation matches when every footprint offset lands on a target-background zero cell; unoccupied cells inside the footprint's bounding box do not constrain the match. Recolor the matched footprint cells to the seed color, taking the union if matches overlap. Preserve all other cells, including noise, nonzero background, and the original seed. Infer the target condition and colors from demonstrations rather than assuming them. Validated across examples with seed colors 2, 4, and 8, including a 3-cell L footprint; verify by checking all candidate placements and that only matching footprint cells change.","path":"general","scope":"global","reference":{},"arguments":{},"metadata":{"scope":"global"},"source":"refine","created_at":"2026-09-29T11:45:17.668Z","updated_at":"2026-09-29T12:02:55.620Z","version":4},"applied":true}],"harnessStatePath":"/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-primeagentlearner-1790682263/attempts/f584a00acdda4d60937d1e953c54734b/system/state/agent/harness/harness_state.json","scope":"global"}
{"id":"refine_20260929120315538","summary":"Updated the global enclosed-pocket memory with a validated example of filling multiple cavities, including one connected to an outward stroke.","rationale":"The demonstrated output confirms that both fully enclosed regions are recolored to 4, while the exterior and monochrome pattern remain unchanged; an outward appendage does not open a cavity.","expectedOutcome":"Future ARC solutions should detect and fill all enclosed pockets despite connected exterior strokes, and preserve all non-pocket cells; validate by checking cavity boundaries and the full transformed grid.","appliedEdits":[{"action":"update","kind":"memory","id":"arc_enclosed_pocket_fill_color4","title":"Fill enclosed pockets in ARC line-pattern grids","content":"For ARC-style grids with a uniform background and monochrome line or outline patterns, infer from demonstrations whether enclosed background pockets are recolored to 4. Fill every fully enclosed pocket, including multiple distinct cavities; leave exterior background and all pattern cells unchanged. An outline may join an outward stroke without opening an otherwise closed pocket. In a validated 17×14 example with background 3 and pattern color 6, the pockets at rows 4–5, columns 3–7, and rows 11–12, columns 3–5 (0-indexed) were both filled with 4; one cavity's contour joined a leftward outward stroke. Check connectivity/boundaries for each candidate pocket and verify the complete output, including unchanged exterior cells.","metadata":{"scope":"global"},"reason":"Adds a confirmed multiple-cavity case and clarifies that outward appendages do not invalidate an enclosed pocket.","before":{"id":"arc_enclosed_pocket_fill_color4","kind":"memory","title":"Fill enclosed pockets in ARC line-pattern grids","content":"For ARC-style grids with a uniform background and monochrome line or outline patterns, inspect demonstrations for enclosed background pockets that are recolored to 4. The background and stroke colors can vary; infer the target color and pocket condition from the examples rather than assuming fixed input colors. A cavity may still count as enclosed even when its outline connects to a long stroke extending into the exterior. In a validated example, a 5×5 color-7 outline embedded in color 5 enclosed a 3×3 pocket; the outline connected to a separate long vertical color-7 line extending above and below, but the entire cavity was still recolored to 4. Change only the demonstrated enclosed pocket cells; preserve the outline and exterior background. Validate cavity boundaries and compare every changed cell with the inferred rule.","path":"general","scope":"global","reference":{},"arguments":{},"metadata":{"scope":"global"},"source":"refine","created_at":"2026-09-29T11:59:58.172Z","updated_at":"2026-09-29T12:00:32.537Z","version":2},"after":{"id":"arc_enclosed_pocket_fill_color4","kind":"memory","title":"Fill enclosed pockets in ARC line-pattern grids","content":"For ARC-style grids with a uniform background and monochrome line or outline patterns, infer from demonstrations whether enclosed background pockets are recolored to 4. Fill every fully enclosed pocket, including multiple distinct cavities; leave exterior background and all pattern cells unchanged. An outline may join an outward stroke without opening an otherwise closed pocket. In a validated 17×14 example with background 3 and pattern color 6, the pockets at rows 4–5, columns 3–7, and rows 11–12, columns 3–5 (0-indexed) were both filled with 4; one cavity's contour joined a leftward outward stroke. Check connectivity/boundaries for each candidate pocket and verify the complete output, including unchanged exterior cells.","path":"general","scope":"global","reference":{},"arguments":{},"metadata":{"scope":"global"},"source":"refine","created_at":"2026-09-29T11:59:58.172Z","updated_at":"2026-09-29T12:03:20.174Z","version":3},"applied":true}],"harnessStatePath":"/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs