{"id":"refine_20260929120429381","summary":"Create a global memory for the demonstrated ARC tile-count encoding task family.","rationale":"The user explicitly requested this global memory, and two demonstration pairs support the stated separator-based partitioning and marker-count rule.","expectedOutcome":"Future similar tasks can be solved by partitioning at full separator lines, counting markers in each tile, and encoding the tile counts; validate tile boundaries and each output cell against the demonstrations.","appliedEdits":[{"action":"create","kind":"memory","id":"arc_partitioned_tile_marker_count_encoding","title":"Encode marker counts in separator-partitioned ARC tiles","content":"For this demonstrated ARC task family, full-width and full-height separator lines partition the grid into rectangular tiles. Each tile has a background color and sparse marker cells of another color. Produce one output cell per tile, preserving the tile layout: a tile with exactly two markers maps to color 1; a tile with exactly one marker maps to that tile's background color. Demonstrations support the rule: in one example the background is 2 and a one-marker tile maps to 2, while in another the background is 9 and one-marker tiles map to 9; two-marker tiles map to 1. Determine tile boundaries from the separators rather than assuming fixed tile sizes, count markers tile by tile, and verify output dimensions and every encoded cell.","metadata":{"scope":"global"},"reason":"Captures the user-requested reusable rule, supported by two demonstrations, for separator-partitioned grids.","after":{"id":"arc_partitioned_tile_marker_count_encoding","kind":"memory","title":"Encode marker counts in separator-partitioned ARC tiles","content":"For this demonstrated ARC task family, full-width and full-height separator lines partition the grid into rectangular tiles. Each tile has a background color and sparse marker cells of another color. Produce one output cell per tile, preserving the tile layout: a tile with exactly two markers maps to color 1; a tile with exactly one marker maps to that tile's background color. Demonstrations support the rule: in one example the background is 2 and a one-marker tile maps to 2, while in another the background is 9 and one-marker tiles map to 9; two-marker tiles map to 1. Determine tile boundaries from the separators rather than assuming fixed tile sizes, count markers tile by tile, and verify output dimensions and every encoded cell.","path":"general","scope":"global","reference":{},"arguments":{},"metadata":{"scope":"global"},"source":"refine","created_at":"2026-09-29T12:04:38.542Z","updated_at":"2026-09-29T12:04:38.542Z","version":1},"applied":true}],"harnessStatePath":"/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-primeagentlearner-1790682263/attempts/f584a00acdda4d60937d1e953c54734b/system/state/agent/harness/harness_state.json","scope":"global"}
{"id":"refine_20260929121134320","summary":"Added a global memory for two-color ARC recoloring based on demonstrated per-color substitutions.","rationale":"The trajectory includes three demonstrations showing position-preserving, uniform color substitutions and a test solved with 9→0 and 5→9; two guesses based on color frequency were incorrect, so the evidence supports avoiding unsupported majority/minority rules.","expectedOutcome":"Future similar tasks should preserve grid geometry, use demonstrated color mappings when available, and request a demo rather than guessing when the mapping is unclear.","appliedEdits":[{"action":"create","kind":"memory","id":"arc_two_color_grid_recoloring_from_demos","title":"Apply demonstrated per-color substitutions in two-color ARC grids","content":"For noisy ARC grids containing two colors, demonstrations in this task family preserve every cell position and apply a uniform substitution to each source color across the whole grid. Observed source-to-output mappings: {5,7}: 5→7 and 7→0; {9,5}: 9→0 and 5→9; {2,5}: 2→0 and 5→2. The {9,5} mapping was confirmed correct on a test grid. Do not infer that the majority color maps to 0 or that colors follow one universal arithmetic formula: a majority/minority-based guess was incorrect in the trajectory, and no general formula is established. When the mapping is uncertain, request a demonstration; then apply its substitutions uniformly and verify dimensions and cell positions.","metadata":{"scope":"global"},"reason":"Preserves validated examples and a failure-avoidance lesson for a recurring ARC transformation family without claiming an unsupported general rule.","after":{"id":"arc_two_color_grid_recoloring_from_demos","kind":"memory","title":"Apply demonstrated per-color substitutions in two-color ARC grids","content":"For noisy ARC grids containing two colors, demonstrations in this task family preserve every cell position and apply a uniform substitution to each source color across the whole grid. Observed source-to-output mappings: {5,7}: 5→7 and 7→0; {9,5}: 9→0 and 5→9; {2,5}: 2→0 and 5→2. The {9,5} mapping was confirmed correct on a test grid. Do not infer that the majority color maps to 0 or that colors follow one universal arithmetic formula: a majority/minority-based guess was incorrect in the trajectory, and no general formula is established. When the mapping is uncertain, request a demonstration; then apply its substitutions uniformly and verify dimensions and cell positions.","path":"general","scope":"global","reference":{},"arguments":{},"metadata":{"scope":"global"},"source":"refine","created_at":"2026-09-29T12:11:39.971Z","updated_at":"2026-09-29T12:11:39.971Z","version":1},"applied":true}],"harnessStatePath":"/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-primeagentlearner-1790682263/attempts/f584a00acdda4d60937d1e953c54734b/system/state/agent/harness/harness_state.json","scope":"global"}
{"id":"refine_20260929121748198","summary":"Updated the global opposite-edge bar memory with a validated disjoint-segment example.","rationale":"The task was solved correctly with color-7 beams from markers toward the adjacent left bar, marker recoloring to 4, and segment-relative offset transfer to rows on the opposite bar.","expectedOutcome":"Future solutions should handle opposite-edge bars on disjoint row segments and transferred rays that cross the grid; validate beam direction, offsets, and preserved bar cells against the geometry and demonstrations.","appliedEdits":[{"action":"update","kind":"memory","id":"arc_opposite_edge_bar_marker_beams","title":"Propagate marker beams between opposite edge bars in ARC grids","content":"For ARC grids with a uniform background, two long straight bars on opposite edges, and sparse marker cells of a third color, infer beam directions and segment-relative offset mapping from the geometry and demonstrations rather than assuming a fixed orientation. Keep the edge bars unchanged. In validated examples, sparse markers are recolored to 4, while each marker sends a straight beam in its original color through the background toward the aligned edge bar; fill only the cells between marker and bar, preserving the bar and cells on the other side. Markers can also select parallel rows or columns on the opposite bar: measure each marker's offset along its source bar segment and copy that offset to the opposite bar's segment, then extend a ray inward from that segment. This can produce strokes in a disjoint row/column segment, including across the grid to the far edge where no bar is present. Preserve all bar cells and unselected background cells. Validated variant: on an 11x7 grid with background 5, color-0 left bar at column 0 rows 4–6, color-0 right bar at column 6 rows 0–2, and color-7 markers at (4,3) and (5,3), recolor markers to 4; fill color 7 at columns 1–2 of rows 4–5 toward the left bar; marker offsets 0 and 1 within the left segment select rows 0 and 1 within the right segment, where color 7 fills columns 0–5, preserving the right-bar cells at column 6. Other validated examples establish the same principle for vertical side bars and marker offsets. Confirm orientation, source segment, offset mapping, beam direction, and exact fill extent from examples; do not force this rule onto differently arranged bars without evidence.","metadata":{"scope":"global"},"reason":"Adds the correctly solved disjoint-segment case and clarifies the need to infer segment-relative offsets and beam direction from geometry.","before":{"id":"arc_opposite_edge_bar_marker_beams","kind":"memory","title":"Propagate marker beams between opposite edge bars in ARC grids","content":"For ARC grids with a uniform background, two long straight colored bars on opposite edges, and sparse marker cells of a third color, infer the beam pattern from the demonstrations:\n\n- Keep the edge bars unchanged. Sparse marker cells are recolored to 4.\n- Each marker sends a straight beam of its own original color through the background toward the aligned edge bar. Fill only cells between the marker and the bar; preserve the bar and cells on the other side of the marker. The ray is horizontal for vertical side bars and vertical for top/bottom bars.\n- The markers also encode which parallel rows/columns are filled from the other edge bar. Measure each marker's offset along its edge-bar segment (e.g. for vertical bars, row - segment_start). Copy that offset to the opposite bar's segment and extend a ray inward, across the grid, in the marker color. This can create complete strokes in a region with no original marker. Preserve edge-bar cells and all unselected background cells.\n- Verify orientation and which segment serves as the source from examples; don't assume only bar endpoints extend. In the validated vertical-side-bar examples, all marker offsets were copied to matching rows in the opposite edge-bar segment.\n\nValidated instance: 10x6 background 6; left color-5 bar at column 0, rows 0–3; right color-5 bar at column 5, rows 6–9; color-2 markers at (6,1),(7,2),(8,1),(9,1). Output fills color 2 across columns 1–5 on rows 0–3 (copied offsets 0–3 from markers in the right segment). In rows 6–9, each marker becomes 4 and color 2 fills between it and the right bar: row 6 c2–4, row 7 c3–4, rows 8–9 c2–4. Bars/background elsewhere stay unchanged.\n\nThis mechanism is also validated on larger vertical-side-bar examples: marker rows' offsets within one bar segment select horizontal strokes in the opposite segment, while each original marker emits a beam toward its adjacent border. For diagonal or differently arranged bars, confirm the alignment/offset mapping instead of forcing this rule.","path":"arc","scope":"global","reference":{},"arguments":{},"metadata":{"scope":"general","version":1,"validated_examples":3},"source":"agent","created_at":"2026-09-29T12:09:02.669400+00:00","updated_at":"2026-09-29T12:09:02.669422+00:00","version":1},"after":{"id":"arc_opposite_edge_bar_marker_beams","kind":"memory","title":"Propagate marker beams between opposite edge bars in ARC grids","content":"For ARC grids with a uniform background, two long straight bars on opposite edges, and sparse marker cells of a third color, infer beam directions and segment-relative offset mapping from the geometry and demonstrations rather than assuming a fixed orientation. Keep the edge bars unchanged. In validated examples, sparse markers are recolored to 4, while each marker sends a straight beam in its original color through the background toward the aligned edge bar; fill only the cells between marker and bar, preserving the bar and cells on the other side. Markers can also select parallel rows or columns on the opposite bar: measure each marker's offset along its source bar segment and copy that offset to the opposite bar's segment, then extend a ray inward from that segment. This can produce strokes in a disjoint row/column segment, including across the grid to the far edge where no bar is present. Preserve all bar cells and unselected background cells. Validated variant: on an 11x7 grid with background 5, color-0 left bar at column 0 rows 4–6, color-0 right bar at column 6 rows 0–2, and color-7 markers at (4,3) and (5,3), recolor markers to 4; fill color 7 at columns 1–2 of rows 4–5 toward the left bar; marker offsets 0 and 1 within the left segment select rows 0 and 1 within the right segment, where color 7 fills columns 0–5, preserving the right-bar cells at column 6. Other validated examples establish the same principle for vertical side bars and marker offsets. Confirm orientation, source segment, offset mapping, beam direction, and exact fill extent from examples; do not force this rule onto differently arranged bars without evidence.","path":"arc","scope":"global","reference":{},"arguments":{},"metadata":{"scope":"global"},"source":"refine","created_at":"2026-09-29T12:09:02.669400+00:00","updated_at":"2026-09-29T12:17:56.180Z","version":2},"applied":true}],"harnessStatePath":"/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-primeagentlearner-1790682263/attempts/f584a00acdda4d60937d1e953c54734b/system/state/agent/harness/harness_state.json","scope":"global"}
