{"id":"refine_20260929122733389","summary":"Updated the global elbow-guided ARC memory with a demonstrated repeated-footprint selection rule and a caution against choosing targets by proximity alone.","rationale":"In the solved instance, the elbow’s color was applied to one of two components with identical normalized footprints; the exterior-side copy was recolored while its counterpart was preserved. Earlier distance-based guesses were incorrect.","expectedOutcome":"For similar ARC tasks, solutions should compare component shapes and translations, then use the elbow’s concave-side orientation to distinguish which matching copy to recolor; validate that the other copy and unrelated cells remain unchanged.","appliedEdits":[{"action":"update","kind":"memory","id":"arc_elbow_guided_component_recoloring","title":"Infer component recoloring from large elbow-shaped color cues (general, v3)","content":"For ARC grids with a large monochrome L/elbow object, use its color as the paint color for one other entire component. Infer the target from all demonstrations rather than assuming the nearest component is selected. In a demonstrated case, identify repeated non-background components with identical normalized footprints (same cell offsets from their bounding-box origins, preserving orientation), then use the elbow’s concave quadrant and the components’ relative placement to determine which copy is targeted: recolor the matching copy on the exterior side of the elbow’s concave quadrant, opposite the inside of the L, while preserving its identical counterpart on the inside. A solved example used a color-1 elbow and two identical color-3 footprints at rows 13–16/columns 10–15 and rows 21–24/columns 23–28; the upper-left, exterior-side copy was recolored 1 and the other was unchanged. Caution: a plausible nearby component selected only by distance is not sufficient. Compare component footprints across the grid, establish their relative placement to the elbow, and verify the rule against every demonstration before applying it. Preserve all cells outside the selected component.","metadata":{"scope":"global"},"reason":"The solved instance validates a more specific footprint-and-elbow-relative selection rule and demonstrates that earlier proximity-based target guesses are unreliable.","before":{"id":"arc_elbow_guided_component_recoloring","kind":"memory","title":"Infer component recoloring from large elbow-shaped color cues","content":"For ARC grids with a large monochrome L/elbow object, use its color as the paint color for one other entire component. Infer the target from all demonstrations: compare component footprints (including exact translations of repeated motifs) and their positions relative to the elbow corner and arms. The diagonal/opposite-quadrant relation can help, but is not unconditional; do not recolor every matching copy. Change only the inferred target component and preserve all other cells.\n\nValidated task-7347b058: in the test, color 2 was the large elbow. Recolor only the 4x3 zero component at rows 2-5, columns 16-18 (zero-based; footprint rows 111,111,111,110) to 2. Its footprint matches the color-8 component at rows 0-3, columns 24-26, which stays 8. This solved the test. The key was comparing exact footprints and using their positions relative to the elbow to choose which copy changes. Validate output dimensions and row lengths before submitting.","path":"general","scope":"global","reference":{},"arguments":{},"metadata":{"evidence":"Validated by correct submission on task-7347b058 after 8 demonstrations; exact matched 4x3 footprint and recolored target documented."},"source":"agent","created_at":"2026-09-29T11:55:26.707Z","updated_at":"2026-09-29T12:17:11.514829+00:00","version":2},"after":{"id":"arc_elbow_guided_component_recoloring","kind":"memory","title":"Infer component recoloring from large elbow-shaped color cues (general, v3)","content":"For ARC grids with a large monochrome L/elbow object, use its color as the paint color for one other entire component. Infer the target from all demonstrations rather than assuming the nearest component is selected. In a demonstrated case, identify repeated non-background components with identical normalized footprints (same cell offsets from their bounding-box origins, preserving orientation), then use the elbow’s concave quadrant and the components’ relative placement to determine which copy is targeted: recolor the matching copy on the exterior side of the elbow’s concave quadrant, opposite the inside of the L, while preserving its identical counterpart on the inside. A solved example used a color-1 elbow and two identical color-3 footprints at rows 13–16/columns 10–15 and rows 21–24/columns 23–28; the upper-left, exterior-side copy was recolored 1 and the other was unchanged. Caution: a plausible nearby component selected only by distance is not sufficient. Compare component footprints across the grid, establish their relative placement to the elbow, and verify the rule against every demonstration before applying it. Preserve all cells outside the selected component.","path":"general","scope":"global","reference":{},"arguments":{},"metadata":{"scope":"global"},"source":"refine","created_at":"2026-09-29T11:55:26.707Z","updated_at":"2026-09-29T12:27:40.143Z","version":3},"applied":true}],"harnessStatePath":"/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-primeagentlearner-1790682263/attempts/f584a00acdda4d60937d1e953c54734b/system/state/agent/harness/harness_state.json","scope":"global"}
{"id":"refine_20260929122813239","summary":"Create a global memory for an ARC transformation that expands a grid into three vertically stacked bands and two horizontally mirrored copies.","rationale":"A demonstration confirmed the exact 3H×2W layout, and the rule produced a correct test submission with no wrong attempts.","expectedOutcome":"Future instances with this transformation can be solved by copying cells unchanged into the specified mirrored layout; validate output dimensions and row and band ordering.","appliedEdits":[{"action":"create","kind":"memory","id":"arc_three_band_horizontal_mirror_tiling","title":"Expand ARC grids into three vertically stacked, horizontally mirrored bands","content":"For the demonstrated ARC task family, expand an input grid of height H and width W into a 3H×2W output. For each source row, construct a 2W row by concatenating the source row reversed with the original source row. Stack three H-row bands: first the source rows in reverse order, then in forward order, then in reverse order. Copy all cell values unchanged; only the layout changes. Validate dimensions, each row's reversed-left/original-right halves, and the three band orderings against demonstrations.","metadata":{"scope":"global"},"reason":"The demonstrated transformation was validated by a correct test-grid submission.","after":{"id":"arc_three_band_horizontal_mirror_tiling","kind":"memory","title":"Expand ARC grids into three vertically stacked, horizontally mirrored bands","content":"For the demonstrated ARC task family, expand an input grid of height H and width W into a 3H×2W output. For each source row, construct a 2W row by concatenating the source row reversed with the original source row. Stack three H-row bands: first the source rows in reverse order, then in forward order, then in reverse order. Copy all cell values unchanged; only the layout changes. Validate dimensions, each row's reversed-left/original-right halves, and the three band orderings against demonstrations.","path":"general","scope":"global","reference":{},"arguments":{},"metadata":{"scope":"global"},"source":"refine","created_at":"2026-09-29T12:28:18.001Z","updated_at":"2026-09-29T12:28:18.001Z","version":1},"applied":true}],"harnessStatePath":"/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-primeagentlearner-1790682263/attempts/f584a00acdda4d60937d1e953c54734b/system/state/agent/harness/harness_state.json","scope":"global"}
