 tiles","content":"For this demonstrated ARC task family, full-width and full-height separator lines partition the grid into rectangular tiles. Each tile has a background color and sparse marker cells of another color. Produce one output cell per tile, preserving the tile layout: a tile with exactly two markers maps to color 1; a tile with exactly one marker maps to that tile's background color. Demonstrations support the rule: in one example the background is 2 and a one-marker tile maps to 2, while in another the background is 9 and one-marker tiles map to 9; two-marker tiles map to 1. Determine tile boundaries from the separators rather than assuming fixed tile sizes, count markers tile by tile, and verify output dimensions and every encoded cell.","path":"general","scope":"global","reference":{},"arguments":{},"metadata":{"scope":"global"},"source":"refine","created_at":"2026-09-29T12:04:38.542Z","updated_at":"2026-09-29T12:04:38.542Z","version":1},"after":{"id":"arc_partitioned_tile_marker_count_encoding","kind":"memory","title":"Encode marker counts in separator-partitioned ARC tiles","content":"For ARC grids partitioned by full-width and full-height separator lines, treat each rectangular region between separators as a tile and produce one output cell per tile. Identify each tile's background color and sparse marker color from the grid, then count marker cells. In validated examples, a tile with exactly two markers maps to output color 1, while a tile with exactly one marker maps to that tile's background color (which may vary by task, such as 5 or 3). Do not hard-code color 5 for one-marker tiles. Infer any other count-to-output mappings from demonstrations rather than extrapolating. Example: background 3, marker 2, tile counts [[2,2],[1,1],[2,1]] produced [[1,1],[3,3],[1,3]]. Validate separator boundaries, per-tile counts, and output shape against the demonstrations.","path":"general","scope":"global","reference":{},"arguments":{},"metadata":{"scope":"globa