{"id":"refine_20260929125843444","summary":"Created a global memory for extracting compressed outputs from separator-grid junction patterns.","rationale":"The demonstrations and confirmed test result support sampling junction colors, trimming consecutive uniform boundary rows or columns, and converting each remaining 2×2 junction block to either its shared color or the interior background color.","expectedOutcome":"Future instances of this task family should produce the correct compressed grid dimensions and cell values; validate by checking sampled junctions, boundary trimming, and every overlapping 2×2 block against the demonstrations.","appliedEdits":[{"action":"create","kind":"memory","id":"arc_separator_grid_junction_block_encoding_f362c6ff","title":"Compress separator-grid junctions using uniform 2×2 blocks (task-f362c6ff)","content":"For ARC tasks like task-f362c6ff, where a tiled grid has horizontal and vertical separator lines with distinctive colors at their intersections, sample the junction colors into a smaller matrix. Trim consecutive boundary rows or columns of this junction matrix that are uniformly the separator/background line color. Then examine every overlapping 2×2 block of remaining junctions: output the shared color when all four corners match; otherwise output the interior tile/background color. The result has one fewer row and column than the trimmed junction matrix. Infer the actual separator and interior colors from the input and demonstrations; do not assume fixed color values. Validate junction coordinates, trim only qualifying boundary rows/columns, and check each 2×2 block. In the confirmed task-f362c6ff test, the junction matrix was [[6,6,8,8],[6,6,8,8]]; trimming the two rightmost all-8 columns left [[6,6]], producing [[6]], which was correct. Demonstrations also support mixed blocks taking the interior color.","metadata":{"scope":"global","project":"Continual-ARC","evidence":"Demonstrations 3–7 and correct task-f362c6ff test submission"},"reason":"Preserves the demonstrated reusable extraction and encoding rule, including repeated boundary trimming and the confirmed test outcome.","after":{"id":"arc_separator_grid_junction_block_encoding_f362c6ff","kind":"memory","title":"Compress separator-grid junctions using uniform 2×2 blocks (task-f362c6ff)","content":"For ARC tasks like task-f362c6ff, where a tiled grid has horizontal and vertical separator lines with distinctive colors at their intersections, sample the junction colors into a smaller matrix. Trim consecutive boundary rows or columns of this junction matrix that are uniformly the separator/background line color. Then examine every overlapping 2×2 block of remaining junctions: output the shared color when all four corners match; otherwise output the interior tile/background color. The result has one fewer row and column than the trimmed junction matrix. Infer the actual separator and interior colors from the input and demonstrations; do not assume fixed color values. Validate junction coordinates, trim only qualifying boundary rows/columns, and check each 2×2 block. In the confirmed task-f362c6ff test, the junction matrix was [[6,6,8,8],[6,6,8,8]]; trimming the two rightmost all-8 columns left [[6,6]], producing [[6]], which was correct. Demonstrations also support mixed blocks taking the interior color.","path":"general","scope":"global","reference":{},"arguments":{},"metadata":{"scope":"global","project":"Continual-ARC","evidence":"Demonstrations 3–7 and correct task-f362c6ff test submission"},"source":"refine","created_at":"2026-09-29T12:58:51.336Z","updated_at":"2026-09-29T12:58:51.336Z","version":1},"applied":true}],"harnessStatePath":"/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-primeagentlearner-1790682263/attempts/f584a00acdda4d60937d1e953c54734b/system/state/agent/harness/harness_state.json","scope":"global"}
