{"id":"refine_20260929131144597","summary":"Updated the global task-f362c6ff memory with the validated junction sampling, boundary trimming, and 2×2 block-reduction rule.","rationale":"The successful submission confirms that junctions must be sampled across separator boundaries, only qualifying outer rows or columns are trimmed, and overlapping 2×2 blocks—not the sampled matrix itself—determine the output.","expectedOutcome":"Future instances should reproduce the validated transformation and avoid direct submission of the junction matrix; validate separator coordinates, trimming, and each block’s output against the rule.","appliedEdits":[{"action":"update","kind":"memory","id":"arc_separator_grid_junction_block_encoding_f362c6ff","title":"Compress separator-grid junctions using uniform 2×2 blocks (task-f362c6ff)","content":"For ARC tasks like task-f362c6ff, sample the colors at every horizontal/vertical separator intersection, including boundary lines, to form a junction matrix. Remove only contiguous outermost rows or columns whose entries are all the separator/background color; do not trim uniform rows or columns in the interior. For each overlapping 2×2 block of the remaining matrix, output its common color if all four junctions match; otherwise output the interior tile/background color. The output has one fewer row and column than the trimmed junction matrix. Infer colors from the input and demonstrations. Validated example: junction columns c=[2,8,14,20,26] and rows r=[0,3,6,9,12,15,18,21]. After removing the top and bottom all-4 rows, 2×2 block reduction yields [[7,7,0,0],[5,7,7,7],[5,5,7,9],[7,5,7,9],[7,5,7,9]]. Directly submitting a sampled junction matrix is incorrect; perform the overlapping-block reduction. Another confirmed example used junction matrix [[6,6,8,8],[6,6,8,8]]; trimming the two rightmost all-8 columns left [[6,6]], producing [[6]]. Demonstrations also support mixed blocks taking the interior color. Validate sampling coordinates, trim only qualifying outer boundaries, and check every output block.","path":"general","metadata":{"scope":"global"},"reason":"The latest task instance provides a successful, concrete example that clarifies junction coordinates, boundary-only trimming, and block reduction.","before":{"id":"arc_separator_grid_junction_block_encoding_f362c6ff","kind":"memory","title":"Compress separator-grid junctions using uniform 2×2 blocks (task-f362c6ff)","content":"For ARC tasks like task-f362c6ff, where a tiled grid has horizontal and vertical separator lines with distinctive colors at their intersections, sample the junction colors into a smaller matrix. Trim consecutive boundary rows or columns of this junction matrix that are uniformly the separator/background line color. Then examine every overlapping 2×2 block of remaining junctions: output the shared color when all four corners match; otherwise output the interior tile/background color. The result has one fewer row and column than the trimmed junction matrix. Infer the actual separator and interior colors from the input and demonstrations; do not assume fixed color values. Validate junction coordinates, trim only qualifying boundary rows/columns, and check each 2×2 block. In the confirmed task-f362c6ff test, the junction matrix was [[6,6,8,8],[6,6,8,8]]; trimming the two rightmost all-8 columns left [[6,6]], producing [[6]], which was correct. Demonstrations also support mixed blocks taking the interior color.","path":"general","scope":"global","reference":{},"arguments":{},"metadata":{"scope":"global","project":"Continual-ARC","evidence":"Demonstrations 3–7 and correct task-f362c6ff test submission"},"source":"refine","created_at":"2026-09-29T12:58:51.336Z","updated_at":"2026-09-29T12:58:51.336Z","version":1},"after":{"id":"arc_separator_grid_junction_block_encoding_f362c6ff","kind":"memory","title":"Compress separator-grid junctions using uniform 2×2 blocks (task-f362c6ff)","content":"For ARC tasks like task-f362c6ff, sample the colors at every horizontal/vertical separator intersection, including boundary lines, to form a junction matrix. Remove only contiguous outermost rows or columns whose entries are all the separator/background color; do not trim uniform rows or columns in the interior. For each overlapping 2×2 block of the remaining matrix, output its common color if all four junctions match; otherwise output the interior tile/background color. The output has one fewer row and column than the trimmed junction matrix. Infer colors from the input and demonstrations. Validated example: junction columns c=[2,8,14,20,26] and rows r=[0,3,6,9,12,15,18,21]. After removing the top and bottom all-4 rows, 2×2 block reduction yields [[7,7,0,0],[5,7,7,7],[5,5,7,9],[7,5,7,9],[7,5,7,9]]. Directly submitting a sampled junction matrix is incorrect; perform the overlapping-block reduction. Another confirmed example used junction matrix [[6,6,8,8],[6,6,8,8]]; trimming the two rightmost all-8 columns left [[6,6]], producing [[6]]. Demonstrations also support mixed blocks taking the interior color. Validate sampling coordinates, trim only qualifying outer boundaries, and check every output block.","path":"general","scope":"global","reference":{},"arguments":{},"metadata":{"scope":"global"},"source":"refine","created_at":"2026-09-29T12:58:51.336Z","updated_at":"2026-09-29T13:11:53.228Z","version":2},"applied":true}],"harnessStatePath":"/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-primeagentlearner-1790682263/attempts/f584a00acdda4d60937d1e953c54734b/system/state/agent/harness/harness_state.json","scope":"global"}
