tenated with the unchanged row). Build the output row sequence as these transformed rows in reverse input-row order, then in normal input-row order, then in reverse input-row order again. Equivalently the three vertical bands are horizontal-mirror expansions of A vertically flipped, A, and A vertically flipped. This exactly matches the 8×6→24×12 demo and the 1×5→3×10 demo. For the test all-2 grid, this yields a 6×6 grid filled with 2. Do not confuse the duplicated mirrored structure with simple replication: each row doubles horizontally and the whole input pattern occupies three vertical bands.","path":"arc","scope":"global","reference":{},"arguments":{},"metadata":{"task_id":"task-2ac99141","version":1},"source":"agent","created_at":"2026-09-29T13:05:40.402984+00:00","updated_at":"2026-09-29T13:05:40.403006+00:00","version":1},"after":{"id":"arc_three_band_vertical_palindrome_with_horizontal_mirror_concatenation_task_2ac","kind":"memory","title":"ARC three-band vertical palindrome with horizontal mirror concatenation (task-2ac99141)","content":"For this ARC task family, demonstrations show an output of size 3H×2W from an H×W input A. For each source row r, form r[::-1] + r (horizontal reflection concatenated with the unchanged row), and build the three vertical bands using the ordering established by the demonstrations. A validated test input [[2],[2]] produced a 6×2 grid of all 2s, confirming 3H×2W expansion and the width-1 row duplication behavior. This symmetric test does not distinguish vertical band ordering; do not infer or alter that ordering from it.","path":"arc","scope":"global","reference":{},"arguments":{},"metadata":{"scope":"global"},"source":"refine","created_at":"2026-09-29T13:05:40.402984+00:00","updated_at":"2026-09-29T13:12:31.703Z","version":2},"applied":true}],"harnessStatePath":"/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-primeagentlearner-1790682263/attempts/f584a00acdda4d60937d1e953c54734b/system/state/agent/harness/harness_st