{"id":"refine_20260929134154914","summary":"Updated the global task-8ceb4c33 memory with the validated marker-centered stamping rule and a coordinate-alignment check.","rationale":"The accepted output confirms that the seed’s full bounding-box pattern is stamped in the same orientation, centered on each isolated marker, while retaining the seed. The incorrect attempt was caused by a one-column transcription shift, not by the inferred transformation.","expectedOutcome":"Future instances should infer the background, template, and marker from the current grid, stamp the complete template including background-colored cells, and verify every stamp’s anchor coordinates before submission.","appliedEdits":[{"action":"update","kind":"memory","id":"arc_three_cell_template_marker_stamping_8ceb4c33","title":"Stamp the full demonstrated motif at markers (task-8ceb4c33)","content":"For task-8ceb4c33 variants, identify the uniform background, seed/template, and isolated target markers from the current grid. Take the seed's non-background bounding box as the complete template rectangle; its geometric center is the anchor, which may be a foreground-colored or background-colored cell. Keep the original seed and stamp the full rectangle in the same orientation centered on each isolated marker, replacing that marker with the template value at the anchor. Copy background-colored cells inside the template too; treat color 0 as foreground whenever it differs from the background. Infer colors, shape, orientation, background, and marker color anew for each instance rather than reusing a previous motif. Validated instance: background 6, template [[1,0,6],[0,3,0],[9,6,6]] with anchor 3, and nine isolated color-5 markers; stamping centered on each marker was accepted. Execution caution: check each marker's exact row/column and anchor alignment when generating or transcribing the output. A one-column shift in one stamp caused an incorrect submission; the aligned grid was accepted.","metadata":{"scope":"global"},"reason":"Adds validated anchor and full-template semantics to the existing task-family memory and records the demonstrated transcription pitfall.","before":{"id":"arc_three_cell_template_marker_stamping_8ceb4c33","kind":"memory","title":"Stamp the full demonstrated motif at markers (task-8ceb4c33)","content":"For task-8ceb4c33, demonstrations show isolated color-8 markers on a uniform background replaced by copies of the entire non-background motif, aligned so each marker occupies the motif's central anchor. Infer the motif and anchor from the source object and demonstrations; preserve the source motif and background. In a validated instance, the background was 3 and the motif occupied a 5×5 bounding box (input rows 5–9, columns 5–9, zero-based). Its anchor was color 0 at offset (2,2). The motif's non-background cells by offset were: (0,0)=2, (0,3)=6, (0,4)=7; (1,0)=0, (1,1)=4, (1,2)=4, (1,3)=2, (1,4)=2; (2,0)=2, (2,1)=6, (2,2)=0, (2,3)=4; (3,1)=7, (3,2)=7, (3,3)=6; (4,2)=2. Include the protruding (4,2) cell even though it may look detached from the main pattern. Stamp at every isolated color-8 marker, replacing each marker with the anchor color and translating every motif cell by the same anchor-relative offsets. Preserve unrelated cells and check for overlaps or boundary clipping as supported by demonstrations. Before submission, verify the output has the intended dimensions, every row has equal width, and all values are valid grid colors; a hand-written output in this instance was rejected as an invalid ARC grid, while a correctly formatted output solved it.\n\nAdditional validated task-8ceb4c33 variants (2026-09-29): A demo had background 5 and a vertical 5×1 seed [3,6,3,0,0] at rows 7–11, column 4 (zero-based), with color-4 markers. The center anchor was the middle cell, color 3 at offset (2,0); demonstrations confirmed copying the full ordered line centered at every marker, including replacing each marker by 3. A test had background 9, horizontal seed [1,6,6] at row 21, columns 2–4, and color-3 markers; the center anchor was 6 at offset (0,1). Copy [1,6,6] horizontally with its center aligned to each marker, replacing it with 6 and placing 1 to its left and 6 to its right. Preserve the original seed and all background cells outside copied motif cells. The 29×11 test output was accepted. These examples confirm that motif size, orientation, anchor color, and marker color vary; infer the complete seed motif and use its geometric center as the anchor rather than assuming the prior 5×5 shape or fixed colors. Check every target fits and that copies do not conflict.\n\nValidated connected-seed extension variant (2026-09-29): In a 13×19 test with background 1 and isolated color-6 markers, the full connected seed spanned rows 4–8 and columns 10–12 (zero-based), not just its visually dense 3×3 core. Its 5×3 bounding-box motif was [·,4,·] / [·,5,·] / [9,9,3] / [7,5,5] / [·,7,·], where · means background. The geometric-center anchor at offset (2,1) was color 9. Stamp the entire motif at each color-6 marker, replacing the marker with 9; translated copies fit and the original seed remains. The accepted output confirmed this. An initial wrong attempt copied only the central 3×3 area and omitted the contiguous 4 and 5 above it. Key caution: identify the complete connected source object, including narrow connected stems/extensions, before determining its bounding box and geometric-center anchor; do not crop to the most compact-looking subshape. A separate demo for this task showed a 3×1 vertical seed [0,0,9] stamped at color-8 markers with the center 0 aligned to each marker, supporting full-seed replication and geometric-center anchoring across variable shapes/colors.","path":"general","scope":"global","reference":{},"arguments":{},"metadata":{"scope":"global"},"source":"agent","created_at":"2026-09-29T13:13:25.556Z","updated_at":"2026-09-29T13:37:29.167750+00:00","version":4},"after":{"id":"arc_three_cell_template_marker_stamping_8ceb4c33","kind":"memory","title":"Stamp the full demonstrated motif at markers (task-8ceb4c33)","content":"For task-8ceb4c33 variants, identify the uniform background, seed/template, and isolated target markers from the current grid. Take the seed's non-background bounding box as the complete template rectangle; its geometric center is the anchor, which may be a foreground-colored or background-colored cell. Keep the original seed and stamp the full rectangle in the same orientation centered on each isolated marker, replacing that marker with the template value at the anchor. Copy background-colored cells inside the template too; treat color 0 as foreground whenever it differs from the background. Infer colors, shape, orientation, background, and marker color anew for each instance rather than reusing a previous motif. Validated instance: background 6, template [[1,0,6],[0,3,0],[9,6,6]] with anchor 3, and nine isolated color-5 markers; stamping centered on each marker was accepted. Execution caution: check each marker's exact row/column and anchor alignment when generating or transcribing the output. A one-column shift in one stamp caused an incorrect submission; the aligned grid was accepted.","path":"general","scope":"global","reference":{},"arguments":{},"metadata":{"scope":"global"},"source":"refine","created_at":"2026-09-29T13:13:25.556Z","updated_at":"2026-09-29T13:42:03.700Z","version":5},"applied":true}],"harnessStatePath":"/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-primeagentlearner-1790682263/attempts/f584a00acdda4d60937d1e953c54734b/system/state/agent/harness/harness_state.json","scope":"global"}
{"id":"refine_20260929134424210","summary":"Created a global memory for the demonstrated rectangle-cleanup rule in ARC task-bca5d41b.","rationale":"Four demonstrations and the accepted test output show that the task preserves multiple solid rectangles, repairs interior speckles to the rectangle color, and removes stray noise to the background; the first incorrect submission also exposed the risk of truncating a rectangle at an interior speckle.","expectedOutcome":"Future instances can identify and restore rectangle bounds despite interior corruption while preserving separate rectangles and clearing isolated noise; validate candidate bounds against the demonstrations and ensure background gaps remain unchanged.","appliedEdits":[{"action":"create","kind":"memory","id":"arc_noisy_rectangle_restoration_bca5d41b","title":"Restore corrupted monochrome rectangles and remove stray noise (task-bca5d41b)","content":"For ARC task-bca5d41b variants, infer the background from the uniform field, then preserve each solid axis-aligned monochrome rectangle, which may vary in color and size. Non-background speckles of other colors inside a rectangle are corruption: recolor them to that rectangle's color. Isolated noise outside rectangles is restored to the background. Determine rectangle extents using the full candidate rectangular spans; do not let an interior speckle split or truncate a rectangle. Background cells generally indicate true boundaries, so keep separate rectangles distinct where background separates them. In the validated test, the right-side color-1 rectangle extended across a row containing differently colored speckles; include that row and repair the speckles to color 1. Validate all rectangle bounds and preserve background gaps.","metadata":{"scope":"global","task_id":"task-bca5d41b"},"reason":"The rule was supported by four demonstration pairs and a correct test submission; the failed first attempt gives a concrete caution about rectangle extent.","after":{"id":"a